# Knowledge bundle: how to contribute

This folder is an Open Knowledge Format (OKF) bundle, version 0.2 (`okf_version: "0.2"` in
`index.md`). It holds verified facts about how Policy Atlas is built and how it behaves.

- **To read it**, start at [index.md](index.md). It groups the concepts by part of the system.
- **What belongs here:** a durable fact that a change has proven and that no test, hook or command
  already enforces. Not design intent (that is `docs/specs/`), not a copy of the specs, not code.
- **When to add:** in the pull request that changes the code, after review, so the concept matches
  the code that ships.

## Authoring a concept

One concept per `.md` file. The filename is the concept id; do not rename it. Every concept needs
YAML frontmatter:

```yaml
---
type: Convention            # required; your choice (Convention, Invariant, Runbook, ...)
title: short display name   # recommended
description: one-line summary   # recommended; quote it if it has ": " or starts with a backtick
tags: [area, topic]         # recommended
generated:                  # who wrote the current content, and when
  by: human:<id>            # or <agent>/<version>
  at: 2026-01-01
sources:                    # what the claims rest on: code, tests, ADRs, specs
  - resource: backend/src/policy_atlas/core/events.py
    title: append, the sequence retry
---
```

Then a short structural body (headings, lists). Link related concepts with markdown links. Add a
line for the concept to the right group in [index.md](index.md).

`index.md` is reserved (no `type`). There is no `log.md`: git history records changes. Run
`make okf-validate` after editing.
